<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Cache and memory bounds for a shared host.

      ClickHouse sizes its caches from total RAM by default: a 5 GiB mark cache and an
      8 GiB uncompressed cache. Neither is an allocation up front, so they look harmless
      — but they are ceilings the server will grow into as the dataset does, on a box it
      shares with a broker, Redis and four services. Measured here, the mark cache held
      11 MiB and the uncompressed cache was untouched, so the defaults buy nothing and
      cost the headroom everything else needs.
    -->
    <!--
      2 GiB, raised from 512 MiB because the closer's lookups now come HERE.

      The note below used to say the correlation closer was served from Redis. That stopped
      being true when late arrivals began merging rather than replacing: every close pass
      asks ClickHouse which correlation ids already exist and loads the stored records for
      the ones that do. Those filter on correlation_id, which the sort key
      (tenant_id, toDate(window_start), correlation_id) cannot serve without a date, so
      each reads roughly 11 million rows of a 13.7M-row table. Marks are exactly what such
      a scan re-reads, and a cache too small to hold them is re-read from disk every tick.
    -->
    <mark_cache_size>2147483648</mark_cache_size>

    <!--
      Disabled outright. It caches decompressed blocks for repeated point reads, which is
      a workload this platform does not have: the queries are analytical scans over time
      ranges. The OS page cache is the better home for that here anyway: it was holding
      24 GiB when this was measured, against a 31 GiB host.
    -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <!--
      6 GiB, a hard ceiling replacing the default 90%-of-RAM.

      RAISED TO 16 GiB AND PUT BACK, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING. The reasoning for raising it
      was that the closer scans ~11M rows a tick and a query tripping the ceiling fails
      rather than degrades. What that missed is that a ceiling is not headroom: with 16 GiB
      to grow into, total usage climbed to meet it and ClickHouse began killing queries by
      OvercommitTracker. Event detail lookups that had all succeeded under the 4 GiB budget
      started failing within the hour, which an analyst sees as a blank raw payload.

      The lesson is that the ceiling was never the constraint. One point lookup was reading
      2 million rows for one row, and raising the limit bought that query room to waste
      instead of making it stop. It was fixed where it lived, in GetNormalized.

      6 GiB rather than the original 4 GiB only because the mark cache below now accounts
      for 2 GiB of the total. Everything else is unchanged.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>6442450944</max_server_memory_usage>
</clickhouse>
